        #revision
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong density
            of a subset
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq X
            is a measure of how “large” the subset is, or what proportion of
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            one can consider
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to take up.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Density is usually defined for subsets
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq \mathbb{N}
            of the natural numbers, or other countable sets, but the concept generalises to sets
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with an exhaustive countable filtration
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \subseteq F_1 \subseteq F_2 \cdots \subseteq X
            (i.e., one such that
            = succeed ")," do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_{n\in \mathbb{N}} F_n = X
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
            is equipped with a measure
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_n
            satisfying
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_n(F_n) \lt \infty
            and where each inclusion
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n \to F_{n+1}
            is measure-preserving. We call such a filtration and system of measures
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F} = \{F_n,\mu_n\}
            a
            %em measured filtration
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The key example is of a totally ordered countable set
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \{a_1,a_2,\ldots\}
            equipped with the filtration
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n = \{a_1,\ldots a_n\}
            and the counting measure. For instance,
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            could be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p Notice that any finite set can also be given the structure of a measured filtration, with a filtration that stabilises after finitely many steps, and with the counting measure.
          %p
            In the other direction, a
            = succeed "-finite" do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\mu)
            can be equipped (by definition) with a measured filtration by measurable subsets, with the induced measures.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given a measured filtration
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mathcal{F})
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq X
              with the property that
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\cap F_n
              is measurable for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %strong
                %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
                \-density of
                %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is defined to be the number
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi ℱ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_\mathcal{F}(S) = \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{\mu_n(S\cap F_n)}{\mu_n(F_n)} \in [0,1],
            %p if this limit exists.
          %p
            For a finite set
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=\{b_1,\ldots,b_n\}
            equipped with any measured filtration
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            = succeed "-density" do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq B
            agrees with the simple ratio
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |S|/|B|
          %p
            Likewise, for any measure space
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\mu)
            of finite total measure and
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            a measurable subset, we can relate
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi ℱ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_\mathcal{F}(S)
            to
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S)/\mu(M)
            for various choices of measured filtration on
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> DR
            \: does this depend on the choice of filtration?)
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            Density arguments are of importance in counting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+numbers"} prime numbers
            with certain properties, and have shown great utility in proving partial results about the Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curves"} elliptic curves
            (namely that it is true for a set of elliptic curves over
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            with density roughly 67%). In this latter example, there are a number of filtrations on can place on the set of elliptic curves up to isomorphism, which only makes small changes to final calculated densities.
          %p
            Any
            = succeed "-finite" do
              %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            of a set
            %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is equivalent, as a measure, to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measure"} probability measure
          %h2#upper_and_lower_asymptotic_density Upper and lower asymptotic density
          %p
            One can also consider, instead of the
            %em limit
            in the definition of density, which may or may not exist, the
            %em lim sup
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %em lim inf
            %strong upper
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %strong lower (asymptotic) densities
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-finite+measure"} ∞-finite measure
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_density"} Natural density
              (which deals only with the case of sets of natural numbers)
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CE%A3-finite_measure"}
                %math#mathml_7e8bd4b21911a6cc3fcb6f6d506b6828021f18c0_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-finite measure
          %p A great number of other definitions of densities of sets of natural numbers (clearly generalisable to other countable ordered sets) are given at
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord
                OEIS
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/OEIS"} ?
              wiki,
              %a{:href => "https://oeis.org/wiki/Density"} Density
